homeFaqs:
  - q: "How fast can someone come out for tree removal?"
    a: "For a tree on a house, car or driveway, the 24/7 line dispatches a crew as soon as it is safe to work, often within hours. For planned removals, same day service is available in many areas, and most other jobs are booked within a few days."
  - q: "What does tree removal cost?"
    a: "Most residential tree removals land between about $300 and $2,000. Height, trunk diameter, how close the tree is to the house or lines, access for equipment, and whether stump grinding is added all move the price. You get a quote before any cutting starts."
  - q: "Does homeowners insurance pay to remove a fallen tree?"
    a: "Usually yes when a covered event such as wind or lightning drops a tree onto an insured structure like the house, garage or fence. Trees that fall in the open without hitting anything, or trees that were dead and neglected, often are not covered. Photograph everything before work begins and call your insurer early."
  - q: "Do I need a permit to remove a tree on my property?"
    a: "It depends on your city or county. Many places protect large or heritage trees and certain species, and some require a permit only above a set trunk diameter. Dead or hazardous trees are often exempt. Check with your local planning or code office before scheduling a non-emergency removal."
  - q: "Is the stump removed too?"
    a: "Removal leaves a stump a few inches high unless stump grinding is added. Grinding takes the stump below the surface so you can plant or mow over it. Ask for both in one visit; it is cheaper than a second trip."
  - q: "What is included in cleanup?"
    a: "Full cleanup means branches are chipped, logs are hauled or stacked where you want them, and the lawn, driveway and beds are raked and blown clear. If you want to keep firewood or wood chips, say so when you call."
